# Does the set of all sets that don't contain themselves contain itself? Russell's paradox
tags: ["set theory","frege","zermelo-fraenkel","foundations of mathematics"]
teaches: ["concepts/russells-paradox","concepts/axiom-of-separation","history/frege-and-russell"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "Why does a harmless-looking rule for making sets lead to a contradiction? What did Russell's 1902 letter do to Frege's life's work? How did mathematics rebuild set theory so the paradox can't be asked?"
next: {"title":"What is the smallest number you can't describe in a few words? Berry's paradox","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/05-what-is-the-smallest-number-you-can-t.cast"}

## Russell's paradox
sources:
    - id: russell1902
      title: Letter to Frege, 16 June 1902
      authors: Bertrand Russell
      year: 1902
      finding: Russell tells Frege of the contradiction of the class of all classes that are not members of themselves; reprinted in van Heijenoort, From Frege to Gödel (1967).
    - id: frege1902
      title: Letter to Russell, 22 June 1902
      authors: Gottlob Frege
      year: 1902
      finding: Frege replies that the contradiction has shaken the basis on which he intended to build arithmetic (in translation).
    - id: frege1903
      title: Grundgesetze der Arithmetik, vol. II (appendix)
      authors: Gottlob Frege
      year: 1903
      finding: Volume I appeared in 1893; volume II (1903) carries a hastily written appendix on Russell's contradiction and a proposed repair of Basic Law V.
    - id: zermelo1908
      title: Untersuchungen über die Grundlagen der Mengenlehre I
      authors: Ernst Zermelo
      year: 1908
      finding: The first axioms for set theory; the axiom of separation lets a property carve a subset out of a set already given, so Russell's argument shows only that no set contains everything.
    - id: sep_russell
      title: Russell's Paradox (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)
      authors: A. D. Irvine and Harry Deutsch
      year: 2024
      finding: Russell found the paradox in 1901 while examining Cantor's proof that there is no largest cardinal; it was resolved by type theory (Russell, 1908; Principia Mathematica 1910–13) and by Zermelo's axiomatic set theory.
      url: https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/russell-paradox/

