    card title "Does it contain itself?"

A: Here's a question that sounds like  (@draw h_set h_in h_rule@)a riddle: does the set of all sets that don't contain themselves contain itself?

B: It sounds like a word game. Surely nothing serious hangs on it.
    highlight h_in

A: A whole foundation hung on it. A set is just a collection, writte (@draw s1@)n in curly braces; this one holds one, tw (@draw a1@)o and three.

A: And everyone took one rule for granted: (@draw rule@) name any property, and there's a set of exactly the things that have it.

B: That's hardly a rule. That's just what 'set' means.
    highlight rule

A: It felt that way to everyone. Here's our route:  (@point at.ref rule gesture underline@)put that rule to work, watch it break, see what that di (@highlight h_set h_in@)d to one man's life's work, and how mathematics rebuilt.

A: So, the (@draw s2@) set of all  (@draw cat@)cats.

B: And the set itself isn't a cat. No whiskers. (@draw a2@) So, no.

A: Now the (@draw s3@) set of everything that (@draw t_yes t_no@) isn't a cat: chairs, numbers, sets.

    ask "Does the set of all non-cats contain itself? Tap your answer." choose ["t_yes","t_no"] answer t_yes store nc voice b
        right: Yes: the set isn't a cat, so it belongs to itself.
        wrong: Ask the set itself: is it a cat?

    erase t_yes t_no

A: Yes. The set isn't a cat, so it qual (@highlight s3 a3@)ifies for its own membership.
    draw a3

A: Same with the set of (@draw s4@) all sets: it's a set, so it sits insi (@draw a4@)de itself.

B: So most sets are ordinary, and a few od (@highlight a3 a4@)d ones swallow themselves.
    highlight a1 a2

A: Now use the rule once more. Collect  (@draw br@)every set that doesn't contain itself, and c (@draw br_r@)all it R.

    clear

    card title "Is R in R?"

B: Fine. So, is R in R?
    draw rbox

    draw e_in n_in e_out n_out

    ask "Is R a member of itself? Tap In or Out." choose ["n_in","n_out"] judge false store rr voice b

A: Whichever you tapped, follow it. Sup (@highlight n_in@)pose R is in R. But R only takes sets that are not (@draw e_in2 l_out@) in themselves, so R must be out.

B: Then it's out. Which makes it a set that doesn't contain itself.
    highlight n_out

A: Exactly  (@draw e_out2 l_in@)the kind R collects. So it's in.

B: In if  (@draw x_in@)it's out, out if (@draw x_out@) it's in.

    erase tree

A: It's Cantor's diagonal ag (@draw grid@)ain, from the hotel lecture. List sets down the side and across the top, and mark which holds which.

A: R f (@draw diag@)lips the diagonal, s (@draw grow@)o it differs from every set (@draw g_diff@) in the list. Russell found the paradox in 1901, inside exactly that proof.

B: So either logic is broken, or one innocent-looking step isn't innocent.
    highlight grow

    erase grid diag grow g_diff

A: Every step was plain logic ex (@draw rule2@)cept one: the rule that any property makes a set. (@highlight rule2 effect box@) That's the suspect.

B: Like the barber w (@draw barber@)ho shaves every man who doesn't shave himself. W (@draw barber_l@)ho shaves the barber?

A: For the barber the answer is easy: no such barber exi (@erase barber barber_l@)sts. But the rule promised th (@highlight rule2 effect circle@)at a set exists for every property.

    clear

    card title "A letter to Jena"

A: And one man had built everyth (@draw frege@)ing on that promise: Gottlob Frege, in Jena.

A: His Bas (@draw bk_t@)ic Laws of Arithmetic set out to build all of arithmetic from logic. Volume (@draw bk1@) one came out in 1893, and in 1902 volume two (@draw bk2@) was at the printer's.

A: On the sixteenth of June 1902, Bertr (@draw russell@)and Russell, aged thirty, wrote to him: he had  (@draw letter letter_a@)met a difficulty, on just one point.

B: Just the one point. Very polite.
    highlight letter

A: Frege wrote back (@draw reply@) within a week: the contradiction had sha (@draw shaken@)ken the ground he meant to build arithmetic on.

A: He added a hurried fix in  (@draw appx@)an appendix, but the fix failed as well, and in the end he gave up building  (@draw bk_x@)arithmetic on logic alone.

    clear

    card title Rebuilding

B: So how did  (@draw old@)mathematics carry on? It still  (@draw old_x@)needs sets.

A: It kept a rule, but a tamer one. In 1908 Ernst  (@draw new@)Zermelo said a property can't make a set from nothing;  (@draw cA cA_l@)it can only pick members out of (@draw members@) a set you already have.

A: Take a set A with these members. Pick out the ones  (@draw rA@)that don't contain themselves, and call that c (@draw rA_l@)ollection R of A.

B: And ask whether (@draw fork@) R of A contains itself. Same trap?

A: Only if R  (@highlight f_in@)of A is one of A's members. Then the old loop com (@draw f_loop@)es back: in if it's out, out if it's in.

B: So it ca (@draw f_x@)n't be a member of A. Then it isn (@draw rdot rdot_l outA@)'t in itself either, and nothing breaks.

    erase fork f_loop f_x

A: Whatever set you p (@highlight rdot@)ick, something isn't a member of it. The paradox has  (@draw noall@)become a theorem: there's no set of everything.

    erase cA cA_l members rA rA_l rdot rdot_l outA noall

A: Russell tried ano (@draw ty1@)ther way, types: thing (@draw ta1 ty2@)s at the bottom, sets o (@draw ta2 ty3@)f things above, sets  (@draw ty_no@)of sets above that, each set holdi (@highlight ty1 ty2 ty3@)ng only lower floors.

A: Then 'R conta (@highlight ty3 effect circle@)ins itself' can't even be written. But Zermelo's way won: extended by Abra (@erase types ty_no@)ham Frae (@draw zf zf2@)nkel and others, it's wh (@highlight new@)at most of mathematics stands on today.

B: Proved safe, this time?
    point at.ref zf gesture underline

A: Not proved. No contradiction has turned up in over a c (@draw safe@)entury, but by Gödel's theorem it can't prove its own consistency.

    clear

    card title "Does it contain itself?"

A: (@draw table rule_end@) So the broken step was the rule that every property makes a  (@draw rule_end_x@)set. Some collections, like everything, can't be sets, and R was the (@draw rbr@) one that gave it away.

    quiz "A library makes a catalogue of every catalogue that doesn't list itself. Should that catalogue list itself?" voice b
        right: Neither: listed, it breaks its own rule; left out, it qualifies. Like R, no such catalogue can exist.
        wrong: Try each answer and see what the catalogue's own rule then says.
        * Yes, it must list itself
        * Yes, but only in a footnote
        + Neither: it can't exist
        * No, it must leave itself out

B: So, does the set of all sets that don't contain themselves contain itself? There's no  (@draw nosuch@)such set. That's the answer.

