      mp3: 
      ms: 5600
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1xy782c'
    '|a||Every step was plain logic except one: the rule that any property makes a set. That''s the suspect.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7560
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1471ehe'
    '|b||Like the barber who shaves every man who doesn''t shave himself. Who shaves the barber?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4920
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:194gb91'
    '|a||For the barber the answer is easy: no such barber exists. But the rule promised that a set exists for every property.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7920
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:zu1gh4'
    '|a||And one man had built everything on that promise: Gottlob Frege, in Jena.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5520
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:on43kb'
    '|a||His Basic Laws of Arithmetic set out to build all of arithmetic from logic. Volume one came out in 1893, and in 1902 volume two was at the printer''s.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10040
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:176zr6j'
    '|a||On the sixteenth of June 1902, Bertrand Russell, aged thirty, wrote to him: he had met a difficulty, on just one point.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8120
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1xzzt8k'
    '|b||Just the one point. Very polite.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2560
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1wdnpp3'
    '|a||Frege wrote back within a week: the contradiction had shaken the ground he meant to build arithmetic on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6360
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:rhv9ea'
    '|a||He added a hurried fix in an appendix, but the fix failed as well, and in the end he gave up building arithmetic on logic alone.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8400
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1dreg80'
    '|b||So how did mathematics carry on? It still needs sets.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4280
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1vivyiw'
    '|a||It kept a rule, but a tamer one. In 1908 Ernst Zermelo said a property can''t make a set from nothing; it can only pick members out of a set you already have.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:rplobr'
    '|a||Take a set A with these members. Pick out the ones that don''t contain themselves, and call that collection R of A.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7760
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:xilva5'
    '|b||And ask whether R of A contains itself. Same trap?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4000
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:zafx1r'
    '|a||Only if R of A is one of A''s members. Then the old loop comes back: in if it''s out, out if it''s in.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6920
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:rg87kp'
    '|b||So it can''t be a member of A. Then it isn''t in itself either, and nothing breaks.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6400
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1245aat'
    '|a||Whatever set you pick, something isn''t a member of it. The paradox has become a theorem: there''s no set of everything.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1h4qdny'
    '|a||Russell tried another way, types: things at the bottom, sets of things above, sets of sets above that, each set holding only lower floors.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9560
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:tnfjj7'
    '|a||Then ''R contains itself'' can''t even be written. But Zermelo''s way won: extended by Abraham Fraenkel and others, it''s what most of mathematics stands on today.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10560
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:14qy7ku'
    '|b||Proved safe, this time?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2040
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1gbldxe'
    '|a||Not proved. No contradiction has turned up in over a century, but by Gödel''s theorem it can''t prove its own consistency.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8200
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1ux4esz'
    '|a||So the broken step was the rule that every property makes a set. Some collections, like everything, can''t be sets, and R was the one that gave it away.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10520
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1dy22ie'
    '|b||A library makes a catalogue of every catalogue that doesn''t list itself. Should that catalogue list itself?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6800
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:6k0tcd'
    '|b||Neither: listed, it breaks its own rule; left out, it qualifies. Like R, no such catalogue can exist.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7560
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|b||So, does the set of all sets that don''t contain themselves contain itself? There''s no such set. That''s the answer.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7200
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|a||Next: What is the smallest number you can''t describe in a few words? Berry''s paradox':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6280
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||Ask the set itself: is it a cat?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3360
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|b||Try each answer and see what the catalogue''s own rule then says.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3680
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|a||Yes.':
